WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:09.640
On a mouse's back, one kind of hair follicle is fenced by the endings of three kinds

2
00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:15.520
of touch neuron. It is called the awl/auchene hair, and this is in mouse trunk

3
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.720
skin. Each kind sends its own signal to the spinal cord.

4
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:22.080
Each signal has its own speed.

5
00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:24.280
Why build a sense this way?

6
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.280
Each of these kinds ends in lanceolate endings.

7
00:00:29.940 --> 00:00:36.060
A lanceolate ending is an axon branch running along the follicle, parallel to the

8
00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:39.140
hair shaft. It is like one picket in a fence.

9
00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:42.920
Terminal Schwann cells interleave with the pickets.

10
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:45.220
These are non-neuronal cells.

11
00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:51.420
At this hair the three classes are Aβ RA, Aδ, and C.

12
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:54.920
The three classes differ in speed.

13
00:00:55.800 --> 00:01:01.920
In mouse hairy skin, recorded in a preparation of skin, nerve, ganglion and spinal

14
00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:07.680
cord, the Aβ RA neurons conduct at 13.6 metres per second.

15
00:01:08.480 --> 00:01:11.320
The Aδ neurons conduct at 5.8.

16
00:01:12.120 --> 00:01:14.940
The C neurons conduct at 0.58.

17
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.340
The C signal is by far the slowest.

18
00:01:19.083 --> 00:01:23.483
How did anyone learn which endings belong to which neurons?

19
00:01:24.703 --> 00:01:30.663
Li and colleagues labelled the C, Aδ, and Aβ RA classes genetically.

20
00:01:31.623 --> 00:01:33.703
They confirmed their identity by recording.

21
00:01:34.843 --> 00:01:38.043
No mouse line for SA1 neurons existed yet.

22
00:01:38.503 --> 00:01:42.843
So they marked SA1 endings by staining the Merkel cells they contact.

23
00:01:43.923 --> 00:01:46.803
Then they imaged whole mounts of mouse trunk skin.

24
00:01:47.663 --> 00:01:53.623
Guard hairs carry Aβ RA endings, plus endings in a touch dome beside the hair.

25
00:01:54.863 --> 00:02:02.123
Awl/auchene hairs carry Aβ RA, Aδ, and C. Zigzag hairs carry Aδ and C.

26
00:02:03.817 --> 00:02:08.057
Now count, in an idealized patch of 100 follicles.

27
00:02:08.537 --> 00:02:12.657
Use the dominant map only, and leave out field-LTMRs.

28
00:02:13.537 --> 00:02:19.977
The three hair types occur in an approximate ratio of 1 guard, 23 awl/auchene, and

29
00:02:19.977 --> 00:02:25.357
76 zigzag. Aβ RA is found at 24 follicles.

30
00:02:26.177 --> 00:02:28.377
Aβ SA1 is found at 1.

31
00:02:28.997 --> 00:02:31.077
Aδ is found at 99.

32
00:02:31.077 --> 00:02:35.897
C is found at 99. The total is 223.

33
00:02:37.167 --> 00:02:40.527
Is 223 a count of neurons?

34
00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:45.067
No. One neuron contacts many follicles.

35
00:02:45.787 --> 00:02:51.427
A single mouse field-LTMR reached 20 to 180 follicles.

36
00:02:52.167 --> 00:02:55.027
And the ratio counts hairs, not neurons.

37
00:02:55.807 --> 00:02:59.887
So the sum tells you how often each class appears at a hair.

38
00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:05.207
Keep this rule. Endings at a hair are not a count of neurons.
